From 4c75dac9f97db88a20afe990dfea8f03f08ba734 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 06:05:01 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 24/30] test: close the remaining P2MR wallet coverage gaps

Five gaps from the review, all in wallet_p2mr.py and none of them requiring a
production change.

Every sighash type now takes the full collaborative route rather than only
SIGHASH_DEFAULT: sign without finalizing, serialize, deserialize, combine,
finalize and broadcast. The shared helper also checks that a PSBT survives the
serialization round trip byte for byte.

The hand computed size of the single input transaction is pinned in weight
units as well as virtual bytes, the two constants are checked against each
other, and the size the wallet estimated is recovered from the fee it charged
and compared with the constant.

Size estimation is now measured per input type rather than only exercised.
Legacy, P2SH-SegWit, P2WPKH, P2TR and P2MR inputs are each spent alone and then
all five together; in each case the estimate must cover the signed transaction
and must not overshoot it by more than two virtual bytes per input. The lower
bound is what catches an underpaid fee. Measured on regtest, the estimate is
exact for every type except P2TR, which is one virtual byte over because it
assumes a 65 byte signature where SIGHASH_DEFAULT produces 64.

The poisoned control block case gains a second malformed candidate that is well
formed in shape but rebuilds a different Merkle root, so the check that spending
data is validated against the prevout no longer rests on the parity bit alone.
Both candidates sort ahead of the real control block and are kept distinct per
leaf, since a PSBT keys a leaf script by its control block alone.

Finally, getnewaddress and getrawchangeaddress are checked explicitly, with and
without an address type, to never return a P2MR address after a tmr() import,
along with the imported descriptor being inactive and the change of a real
spend of a P2MR input not being P2MR either.
